  swi_b interface lo 10.70.70.1/32      <- the prefix that exists ONLY behind swi_b
  swi_b ip route 192.168.10.0/24 10.0.61.1   <- so swi_b's reply can get home
  (swi_d port1.0.4 vlan41 10.0.41.1/30 ── swi_a port1.0.4 vlan41 10.0.41.2/30, and
   swi_a interface lo 10.90.90.1/32, exist but are used only by T43861 and by this case's
   floating-static limb)

**🔴 THE STP PROBLEM, AND WHY THE TRIAGE'S REMEDY WAS WRONG.**  The triage's RISK-4 said to give
every routed leg its own VLAN so nothing is blocked.  **RSTP here is single-instance**
(`spanning-tree mode rstp` on all three), so it computes ONE tree over the PHYSICAL topology and
a Discarding port is blocked for every VLAN.  Measured on all three devices before any change
(20:13-20:15 NZDT), with swi_b root:

  swi_d  port1.0.2  Rootport   Forwarding
         port1.0.4  Alternate  Discarding   <- every candidate routed leg was blocked
         port1.0.14 Alternate  Discarding
         port1.0.16 Alternate  Discarding
  swi_a  port1.0.1  Rootport F · port1.0.11 Alternate D · port1.0.4 and port1.0.14 Designated F
  swi_b  Root Id = its own 8000:0000cd423029; sa1, 1.0.1, 1.0.11, 1.0.16 all Designated F

On each swi_d<->swi_a segment the two bridges TIE on root path cost and swi_a wins on the lower
bridge ID (889d... < e01a...), so no VLAN scheme can free swi_d's legs.

**Resolved with two DUT-only, fully reversible changes**, both reverted at the end of group D:
  awplus(config)# spanning-tree priority 0          <- DUT becomes root => every DUT port
                                                       Designated/Forwarding
  awplus(config)# interface port1.0.2 / shutdown    <- else swi_b roots through sa1 (RISK-2)
                                                       and puts port1.0.16 into Alternate
  awplus(config)# interface port1.0.14 / shutdown   <- makes swi_a's DUT link deterministic
                                                       instead of a port-ID tie-break
swi_a and swi_b took NO spanning-tree change and NO port shutdown.  sa1 / vlan 100 /
swi_b port1.0.2,1.0.4,1.0.8 untouched (RISK-2); swi_a config-only, never powered (RISK-1).

STP verified after the change (20:23 NZDT), and **re-read immediately before every counted leg**:
